insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 1, 'Ekene has six wells and its grid has 900 columns. What is the ratio of derived to measured structural data?', '["About 6 to 1, since each well controls the cells within one variogram range of it and those cover most of the grid area.", "About 1 to 1, because the six wells were chosen during appraisal to sample the structure evenly across the accumulation.", "About 150 to 1, which is the normal condition of every reservoir model ever built.", "It cannot be stated, because kriging weights every well at every cell so no cell is more derived than any other one is."]'::jsonb, 2, '894 of the 900 depths are interpolated. Saying that out loud is the point: a model''s structure is a hypothesis with a small number of anchors, and everything downstream inherits it. The weights do vary with distance, which is exactly why cells far from every well are the least constrained.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 2, 'An interpolator must decide three things the data does not determine. Which set?', '["How far the influence of a well reaches, what the surface does far from any well at all, and whether it passes exactly through the data.", "Which wells to include, how to weight their measurement error, and where to place the grid''s origin relative to the map.", "The cell size, the number of layers, and the depth reference the surface is quoted against in the finished deck.", "Whether to grid in time or in depth, which velocity model to use for the conversion, and how to tie the result to the wells."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Range, regional behaviour and exactness at the data. None is determined by six depths, and each visibly changes the map. Cell size and layering are gridding decisions downstream of the surface, and time-to-depth conversion belongs to a seismic workflow this field does not have.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 3, 'Ekene''s kriging uses a spherical model with a range of 1200 m. How does that compare to the well spacing?', '["It is much shorter than it, which is why the surface shows a distinct bullseye around each of the six control points.", "It is comparable with it, since the wells sit between about 700 m and 2200 m apart, which is the usual choice.", "It is much longer than it, which is why the surface is a single smooth dome rather than six separate features.", "It is unrelated to it, because the range is fitted from the variogram of the data rather than chosen from the geometry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A short range hugs each well and drops to the regional mean between them; a long range lets the wells influence each other and produces a broad smooth feature. At 1200 m on this spacing the result is in between, which is what makes the map look like a plausible dome. Six points cannot support a fitted variogram.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 4, 'What does a nugget of zero make the kriging?', '["An unbiased estimator, which means the average of its errors over the whole grid is zero even though individual cells differ.", "A smoother, because with no nugget the covariance at short range is at its maximum and neighbouring estimates are pulled together.", "Unstable, because a zero nugget makes the covariance matrix singular whenever two data points fall very close together.", "An exact interpolator: the surface passes through every data point."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is a strong property and this deck was built to demonstrate it, which makes it a check on the whole chain from well database to TOPS block. A non-zero nugget is what you want when the measurements carry error, because then the surface should pass NEAR the data rather than through it.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 5, 'Five of Ekene''s six wells come back exactly and one does not. What separates them?', '["The five sit at map coordinates that are multiples of 100 m, so with the deck''s half-cell offset they land on cell centres.", "The five were used as kriging control points and the sixth was held back as a blind test of the surface.", "The five lie within one variogram range of another well, so their estimates are supported by neighbours as well as by themselves.", "The five were logged with the same tool and the sixth with an older one, so its top carries a systematic depth shift."]'::jsonb, 0, 'All six went into the interpolation, so none is a blind test and the field has no spare well for one. The deck samples the surface at cell centres, and Ekene-2 at a northing of 1150 m has no cell centre at its position, so it inherits the depth of the nearest one.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 6, 'Two of the five exactly-recovered wells carry a residual of about 2.3e-13 m. What is that?', '["The convergence tolerance of the kriging solver, which stops when successive estimates differ by less than that amount.", "The precision limit of the Acklam approximation used to convert probabilities to normal quantiles inside the estimator.", "The metre to foot and back round trip: the deck stores depths in feet, so reading one back in metres is not exact in binary.", "A genuine structural residual, since simple kriging is exact only in the limit of a zero nugget and this one is very small but finite."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two tenths of a picometre is arithmetic rather than structure. Knowing which of the two you are looking at is the skill: a discrepancy at the thirteenth decimal is floating point and one at the second is a modelling decision. The nugget here is exactly zero.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 7, 'What does the exactness test check, beyond the interpolator?', '["Only the interpolator, since every other step in the chain is a lossless transformation that cannot introduce an error.", "The whole chain at once: the interpolator, the cell indexing, the coordinate offset, the unit conversion and the ordering of the 900 values.", "The interpolator and the variogram parameters, since a wrong range would move the surface at the wells as well as between them.", "The interpolator and the grid dimensions, since a wrong cell count would leave the TOPS array the wrong length and fail to parse."]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the deck''s depth at Ekene-1 came back as 1547.3 m, something between the well database and the TOPS block is wrong, and one test catches all of them. That is the same trick the decline course used with planted Arps parameters and the SCAL course with a planted J-function.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 8, 'Ekene-2 sits at a northing of 1150 m and the deck gives it a depth 0.68 m shallower than its log. Whose limitation is that?', '["The interpolator''s, because simple kriging cannot honour a point that falls between the nodes of its own estimation grid.", "The well database''s, because the surveyed position carries a lateral uncertainty comparable with half a cell at this depth.", "The variogram''s, because a range of 1200 m is far too long to resolve a lateral offset of only 50 m, and a shorter range would have removed the discrepancy entirely.", "The grid''s, because the deck samples the surface only at cell centres and no cell centre lies at that particular well''s mapped position."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Kriging did pass exactly through (2200, 1150); the surface is correct at the well. This is a gridding loss rather than an interpolation loss, and the distinction matters because a finer grid reduces it while a better interpolator does not.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 9, 'Is Ekene-2''s 0.68 m offset a problem?', '["It depends on what the well is for: negligible for volumetrics, possibly material for a completion picked off the model, and systematic for a history match.", "Yes, because any discrepancy between a model and a well log invalidates the model at that location and must be corrected.", "No, because 0.68 m is far below the vertical resolution of the seismic data the surface was ultimately derived from.", "No, because kriging is exact at the well and the deck''s own depth there is therefore correct by construction."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One column out of 900, off by 0.68 m in a 34.6 ft net pay, is well below every other uncertainty. But the layer interfaces below it are shifted too, and a well whose modelled depth is wrong has a modelled pressure wrong by the fluid gradient over that depth, systematically, for the whole run.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 10, 'What are the three responses to a well that misses the lattice?', '["Re-krige with a shorter range, shift the grid origin to capture the well, or accept the offset and widen the tolerance.", "Re-survey the well, re-map the surface, or exclude the well from the interpolation and treat it as a blind check.", "Record it, move the well to the cell centre in the model, or refine the grid locally.", "Shift the whole TOPS array, apply a per-well depth correction in the schedule, or model the well as deviated instead."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Most studies take the first, and the ones that get bitten took it implicitly by not noticing. Moving the well is a small extra idealisation on top of one already made, since wells are points in cells. Refining is expensive and only reduces the offset, because a finer lattice still has centres.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 11, 'The general rule is that a grid error is the local gradient times the distance moved. What bounds the distance?', '["The variogram range, since a point is moved to the nearest cell centre within its own zone of influence.", "Half a cell in each direction, so the error bound can be computed before anything is built.", "One cell in each direction, since a point on a boundary may be assigned to either of the two cells that share it.", "Nothing in general, since a point outside the grid entirely is moved to the nearest cell however far away that is."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That bound applies to wells, to seismic picks, to core plugs and to pressure gauges, and it is the same arithmetic every time. A point outside the grid is not moved at all: it returns no cell, which is the failure mode the Expert tier takes up.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 12, 'Which of these can a block-centred Cartesian grid NOT represent at all?', '["A fault, unless it happens to lie along a cell face where a transmissibility multiplier can approximate it.", "A contact between the layer interfaces, which is why the model rounds it to the nearest cell centre.", "A layer whose permeability differs from its neighbours by a factor of six, which needs corner-point geometry to express.", "A well that crosses several columns, which is why deviated wells require a connection list rather than a layer range."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fault inside a cell is invisible and a fault along a face can only be approximated. The contact is represented, as a staircase; a six-fold permeability contrast between layers is exactly what this grid carries; and a deviated well is handled by intersecting its trajectory.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 13, 'What can a coarse grid be trusted for long before it can be trusted for timing?', '["Pressure, because the average field pressure is an integral over cells and converges quickly with refinement.", "Water cut, because it is a ratio and the resolution errors in the numerator and denominator largely cancel.", "Recovery factor, because it is dimensionless and therefore insensitive to the absolute size of the cells.", "Volume, because an integral is forgiving of resolution whereas gradients and the smallest connected path through the model are not."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The reconciliation in the next module shows a coarse grid reproducing a booked volume to within a tenth of a percent. Rates and breakthrough times depend on gradients and on the smallest connected path, both of which are resolution-sensitive, so a study quoting both with the same confidence has not thought about it.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 14, 'What three questions follow directly for a deck reader inheriting an unfamiliar model?', '["Which simulator it was built for, which version, and whether any of the relevant defaults have changed between that version and the one you are about to run it on.", "Who built it, when, and whether the study it belonged to was ever completed and reviewed by anybody outside the original team.", "What is the cell size and what does it make invisible, where are the wells relative to the lattice, and how many layers span the interval.", "How long it takes to run, how much output it writes, and whether the restart files needed to continue it are still available."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each of the three is one line of arithmetic and each tells you what the model has decided not to represent. The provenance and practical questions matter too and they do not tell you what the grid can and cannot carry.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-where-the-structure-comes-from', 15, 'A colleague says the model has all the wells in it. What is the precise statement?', '["It has a well wherever the well database records one, positioned exactly and completed over the logged interval.", "It has a well in the cell nearest each surface location, coupled through a well index that assumes radial flow into a 100 m cell.", "It has a well for each producer and injector, with the observation wells omitted from the model entirely because they carry no rates for the schedule to honour.", "It has a well in every cell the wellbore passes through, which for a vertical well is one cell per layer of the column."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is a model of a well, and its relationship to the steel in the ground is worth stating rather than assuming. A cell is 100 m across and the well is a point somewhere inside it, at none of the cell''s single pressure, saturation or permeability in particular.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 1, 'The deck''s oil volume and the NG5 booking were computed by the same engine on the same contact, porosity and saturation. What does that buy?', '["A guarantee that the two agree, since the engine is deterministic and the inputs it was given are identical in both cases.", "Every remaining difference between them is geometry or convention, and nothing else, so the residual is attributable.", "An independent check on the deck, since agreement between two runs of the same engine confirms the model is correct.", "A saving in effort, since the booking''s result can be reused directly rather than recomputing it on the deck''s own frame."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is a general principle for reconciliations: hold constant everything you can, so the residual is attributable. The inputs are NOT identical, because the surfaces and the frames differ, and running the same engine twice is not an independent check of anything.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 2, 'The deck''s STOIIP is 12132366.897955146 stb against a booked 12139208.107496763. Why is a gap of six hundredths of a percent suspicious?', '["Because two independently constructed models do not agree that closely by accident, so a reader should ask what made it happen.", "Because a simulation model should contain rather less oil than a volumetric estimate, so agreement suggests an error in the clipping.", "Because the booking carries only three significant figures of real precision, so agreement at five figures cannot be meaningful.", "Because the two used different frames, so exact agreement would require the deck''s cells to align with the booking''s exactly."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It was arranged: the structure carries one free parameter and it was set so this number would come out. That is a standard step with a standard name, and doing it deliberately and saying so is the honest version. The dishonest version presents the agreement as independent confirmation.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 3, 'Under the Eclipse cell-centre rule, how is a column''s oil contribution decided?', '["By the column''s top: if the top is above the contact the whole column is oil, and otherwise none of it is.", "By the fraction of the column above the contact, applied uniformly to all five layers of that column at once.", "Layer by layer: a layer counts as oil if its own centre depth lies above the contact, so a column contributes whole layers.", "By the capillary pressure curve, which assigns each layer a saturation from its height above the free water level."]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no partial cell, so the modelled oil volume is a staircase whose tread is a cell and whose riser is a layer thickness. The column-top rule would be far cruder, the fractional rule is the tapered convention, and the capillary rule is what a deck with a real Pc curve would do.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 4, 'The column-clipped convention gives 12228351.680153307 stb. How does that compare with the cell-centre answer?', '["It is smaller, because clipping the column removes the parts of each layer that sit below the contact depth.", "It is identical, because both conventions integrate the same oil column and differ only in how they report it.", "It is larger by about eight percent, which is the fraction of columns the contact passes through on this structure.", "It is larger, by about 0.79 percent of the deck''s own volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Clipping the column follows the contact exactly instead of rounding it to layer boundaries, so a column whose top sits a few feet above the contact contributes that few feet rather than a whole layer or none. The gap of 95984.8 stb is a direct measure of vertical resolution at the contact.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 5, 'Which convention should be reported as the model''s oil in place?', '["The cell-centre one, because that is the oil the simulator will actually produce from.", "The column-clipped one, because it follows the contact exactly and is therefore the more physically accurate of the two.", "The booking''s convention, because it is the number the field''s reserves are stated against and consistency matters most.", "The average of the two, because the truth lies between them and neither convention is more defensible than the other."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Report the other alongside it when the gap is material, because the difference tells a reader how resolution-limited the model is. A model whose two conventions agree to a hundredth of a percent is well resolved at the contact; one where they differ by five percent has a contact smeared across a layer thickness that matters.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 6, 'There is a third clipping convention, and it is the one the booking used. What is it?', '["Clip the column at the free water level rather than at the contact, which adds the whole of the capillary transition zone to the booked accumulation as though it were oil.", "Clip the AREA at the contact and give every oil cell its full net thickness, which is standard practice in volumetric mapping.", "Clip at the deepest closing contour of the structure map, which is how a prospect''s volume is bounded before drilling.", "Clip at the lowest known oil in the wells, which is the conservative bound used when the contact has not been seen."]'::jsonb, 1, 'In volumetric mapping the isochore is mapped separately from the structure, so the contact defines the outline of the accumulation rather than the top of the water in each column. All three conventions are defensible and in use, and comparing numbers across them without saying so is what makes this module necessary.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 7, 'Which five quantities were deliberately held fixed between the deck and the booking?', '["Cell size, layer count, grid origin, well positions, and the interpolation method used to make the surface.", "Net-to-gross, permeability, the kriging range, the sill, and the regional mean the surface reverts to far from data.", "Porosity, water saturation, the oil formation volume factor, the contact depth, and the volumetric engine used to sum it all.", "Bubble point, solution gas, oil density, water density, and the temperature the fluid properties were evaluated at."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Holding those five fixed is what makes the comparison meaningful, because every remaining difference is then geometry or convention. The cell size, frame and surface all DIFFER between the two, which is exactly the point; the fluid and rock parameters listed elsewhere do not enter a volumetric calculation at all.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 8, 'The kriging regional mean was tuned so the deck''s volume would land on the booking. Why is that defensible?', '["Because the booked volume is the better-constrained number and the regional mean is constrained by nothing at all.", "Because a simulation model is always calibrated to a volumetric booking, so the practice needs no case-by-case defence.", "Because the regional mean has no effect on the flow behaviour of the model, only on its reported oil in place.", "Because the booking itself was derived from the same six wells, so tuning to it introduces no new information."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The booking came from a workflow built for volumetrics on a frame chosen for it; the deck''s structure exists so a simulator can flow fluid through it and its far-field shape serves no other purpose. Tuning puts the adjustment where the ignorance is. The regional mean very much affects flow, because it decides which cells hold oil.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 9, 'What would have made the calibration indefensible?', '["Tuning the variogram range, since the range is partly constrained by the spacing of the wells that were used.", "Tuning against the column-clipped volume, since that is not the convention the simulator will use when it initialises.", "Tuning by hand rather than by bisection, since a manual search cannot be reproduced by anybody reviewing the study.", "Tuning porosity instead, which would override a measurement with a preference."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Porosity is measured, it appears in the booking, and moving it to fix a volume makes the model wrong in every calculation that uses porosity. The rule is to calibrate the unconstrained parameter against the constrained quantity, never the reverse. Not saying it was tuned at all is the other way to make it indefensible.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 10, 'Why is the match not exact even after tuning?', '["Because the bisection was stopped at a fixed iteration count rather than run to machine precision on the objective.", "Because the oil volume is a step function of the regional mean: columns and layers cross the contact in jumps, so a search cannot land between two steps.", "Because the two volumes are computed in different units and the conversion between them carries a rounding error.", "Because the booking itself is only quoted to seven significant figures, which bounds how closely anything can match it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The residual is a discretisation artifact of the tuning rather than a failure of the search, which is why reporting the match as within a tenth of a percent rather than as an exact figure is the honest phrasing.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 11, 'The deck matches the booked volume over 266 oil cells where the booking used 169. Why can both not be matched?', '["Because the deck''s frame has more cells over the accumulation, so its count is larger whatever the regional mean is set to.", "Because the booking''s 169 cells were counted on a coarser grid, so the two counts are not comparable in the first place.", "Because one free parameter can fix exactly one quantity, and the two models put the same oil into quite different shapes.", "Because the cell-centre rule always produces more oil cells than an area clip, by roughly the ratio of the layer thickness to the relief."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The booking clips area and gives every cell a full isochore; the deck clips by cell centre so a column just above the contact contributes one or two layers. Matching the total means the larger area compensates for the thinner average column. One equation, one unknown.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 12, 'What does the area mismatch cost?', '["Oil in places the flood will not reach, a longer perimeter against the water leg, and a recovery factor that is not comparable with the booking''s.", "Nothing measurable, since the total volume is matched and the distribution does not affect a forecast materially.", "A systematically optimistic forecast, since more oil cells means more oil available to the wells at any given time.", "A loss of vertical resolution, since spreading the same volume over more cells thins each of them below the layer thickness."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The extra 97 cells are a thin fringe around the edge, far from the wells, and oil there is real in the model and largely immobile in practice. The volume is matched, so the model is not optimistic in magnitude; it is differently distributed, and layer thicknesses are unchanged.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 13, 'A colleague reads the cell counts and calls the model optimistic. What is the answer?', '["It is optimistic, but only in the fringe cells, which contribute little and can be excluded from the reported volume.", "It is pessimistic rather than optimistic, because spreading oil into unswept cells lowers the achievable recovery factor.", "It is neither, because oil cell count is not reported in a study and the comparison should not have been made.", "It has the same oil: more cells at less thickness each is the same integral."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Optimism would be more VOLUME, and the volume is matched. Cell count is a statement about distribution rather than magnitude, and reading it as magnitude is exactly what this module exists to prevent. The recovery-factor effect is real and is a consequence rather than the answer to the charge.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 14, 'What are the five lines a reconciliation should record?', '["The two volumes, the two frames, the two interpolators, the two engines, and the date each was computed.", "The two volumes and their difference, the convention each used, what was held fixed, what was tuned, and what it cost.", "The oil in place, the recovery factor, the contact depth, the porosity and the water saturation, with sources for each.", "The deck version, the booking version, the reviewer, the approval date, and the reference to the study document."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every clause is load-bearing. Drop the convention and the comparison is meaningless, drop the calibration and the agreement reads as independent, and drop the cost and the reader will find it themselves and wonder what else was not mentioned.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-volumetric-reconciliation', 15, 'Why is calling this a validation the wrong word?', '["Because validation requires a formal procedure with a written acceptance criterion, which this reconciliation did not have.", "Because validation applies to software rather than to models, so a reservoir model can only ever be verified.", "Because validation is a test the model could have failed, and a match that was deliberately arranged tested nothing at all.", "Because the booking is itself a model rather than a measurement, so neither number can validate the other in principle."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A study that calls its calibration a validation has no remaining independent check on its static model and does not know it. An independent check needs something the model was not tuned to reproduce, and Ekene has no spare well and no independent structural data, which is itself worth writing down.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 1, 'The Ekene deck carries a designed oil rather than a correlated one. What decided that?', '["Every other number about the field was derived with the designed fluid: the tank pressure history, the flood ledger''s frozen factors and the fractional flow curves all used it.", "The designed fluid is closer to the laboratory measurements, which were taken on a sample from Ekene-1 during appraisal.", "Correlations are only valid inside the gravity and temperature range they were fitted over, and Ekene falls outside it.", "The simulator requires a designed table, because correlations cannot be expressed in the PVTO record format at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Consistency across the study beats agreement with a correlation. There is no laboratory PVT for this field, the correlations are perfectly valid at 32 API and 180 F, and a deck carries numbers whatever produced them.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 2, 'Standing''s correlation on Ekene''s gravities gives Rs at the bubble point of 421.93922752270595 scf/stb. What does the design say?', '["421.94 scf/stb as well, since the design was itself derived from the same correlation during the material balance work.", "About 380 scf/stb, which is the value the tank model recovered when it was history matched against the pressure surveys.", "It does not say, because solution gas enters the material balance only through the formation volume factor rather than directly.", "400 scf/stb."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The gap is 5.484806880676496 percent. Read the correlation the other way round and it says an oil of this gravity carrying 400 scf/stb would bubble at somewhat below 2000 psia. Both descriptions are defensible and the deck can carry only one.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 3, 'The correlated formation volume factor at initial pressure is 1.2292846175634324 against a designed 1.2. What would using the correlated value do to the oil in place?', '["Increase it by about 2.4 percent, since a larger formation volume factor means each reservoir barrel holds more stock tank oil.", "Reduce it by about 2.4 percent, because the booking divides reservoir oil by Bo to get stock tank barrels.", "Leave it unchanged, because the booking''s hydrocarbon pore volume is computed before any formation volume factor is applied.", "Change it unpredictably, because Bo enters both the numerator and the denominator of the volumetric expression."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is forty times the reconciliation residual the previous module worked to achieve. A larger Bo means each stock tank barrel occupied more reservoir space, so a fixed reservoir volume contains fewer of them.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 4, 'The correlated oil viscosity at the bubble point is 0.7341185203712621 cp against a designed 1.8. Why is that gap so large?', '["The correlation was evaluated at surface temperature rather than at the reservoir temperature of 180 degrees Fahrenheit.", "Beggs and Robinson is known to underpredict viscosity for oils above 30 API, which is a documented limitation of the method.", "1.8 cp was chosen so the Ekene flood would sit at a mobility ratio of 1.2, which is a realistic and instructive place to be.", "The designed value includes the effect of the dissolved gas while the correlated value is for dead oil at the same gravity."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The SCAL course needed a mobility ratio and set the oil viscosity at flood-era pressure to make the flood mildly unfavourable. Beggs and Robinson on a light oil at a warm reservoir temperature simply says it is thinner. Neither is wrong; the deck must carry the one the flood analysis used.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 5, 'Swapping the designed viscosity for the correlated one would take the mobility ratio from 1.2 to about 0.49. What does that do to the flood?', '["It turns a mildly unfavourable flood into a favourable one, invalidating every sweep number the waterflood course computed.", "It leaves the sweep unchanged, because the mobility ratio affects only the displacement efficiency rather than the areal or vertical sweep.", "It makes the flood slightly more efficient, within the uncertainty of the layered sweep calculation itself.", "It has no effect on the deck, because relative permeability rather than viscosity sets the mobility in a simulation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A PVT substitution nobody would call major, made in one section of one file, would invalidate an entire course''s worth of analysis on the field. Mobility ratio drives the areal sweep correlation, the layered frontal positions and the fractional flow, all at once.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 6, 'What is the rule this module is built on?', '["A deck''s fluid must be the most recently measured description available at the time the deck is built and run.", "A deck''s fluid must be the fluid the rest of the study was matched against.", "A deck''s fluid must come from a correlation, so that the same description can be regenerated by anybody with the gravities.", "A deck''s fluid must be the one that reproduces the observed bubble point, since that is the only directly measured PVT quantity."]'::jsonb, 1, 'If a history match was done with one PVT description and the deck carries another, the match does not transfer and the model reproduces history for the wrong reasons. That is not a tidiness rule, and the previous questions measured what it costs on this field.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 7, 'A new PVT report arrives mid-study and somebody updates the deck alone. What is the symptom, months later?', '["A deck that fails to validate, because the new tables no longer agree with the RSVD value in the SOLUTION section.", "A run that produces different results every time, because the two competing fluid descriptions are being mixed between one report step and the next.", "A model that used to match and no longer does, with no change to the geology, and eventually somebody finds the PVT commit.", "An immediate error at initialisation, since the oil in place no longer matches the value recorded in the study document."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The deck runs perfectly and returns different answers. The volumes drift from the booking, the history match stops reproducing pressures, and the ledger''s frozen factors are stale, and none of it shows up as an error.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 8, 'What does doing a PVT update properly require?', '["Re-running the deck with both descriptions and reporting the spread, which is the standard treatment for a changed input.", "Updating the deck and recording the change in the study document, so the difference from earlier runs is traceable.", "Waiting until the next full model rebuild, since a PVT change mid-study cannot be applied consistently in any case.", "Changing the fluid everywhere or nowhere: re-matching the tank model, recomputing the ledger''s factors, redoing the mobility ratio and refreshing the sweep numbers."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is a substantial piece of work and it is the actual cost of a PVT update, which makes it a project decision rather than a deck edit. Framing it that way is most of the value of understanding it.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 9, 'What does it mean that a history-matched parameter carries the fluid inside it?', '["Any parameter tuned to reproduce an observation absorbed the old fluid''s behaviour, so changing the fluid leaves it compensating in ways nobody intended.", "The parameter''s units depend on the formation volume factor, so its numerical value changes when the fluid does.", "The parameter is stored alongside the PVT tables in the deck, so editing one section automatically invalidates the other.", "The parameter was fitted at a single pressure, so it is only valid for the fluid description evaluated at that pressure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s oil in place was derived from a pressure history through a material balance that used Bo, so it is a function of Bo. The same is true of any history-matched permeability multiplier, aquifer strength or endpoint shift.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 10, 'What three questions should a deck be able to answer about its PVT?', '["Which correlation set was used, at what reservoir temperature it was evaluated, and whether the published validity ranges of each method were respected.", "Where the oil table came from, where the gas table came from, and what else in the whole study was derived using either of them.", "Which laboratory performed the analysis, on which sample, and whether the sample was validated against a second one.", "Which pressures the tables span, how many nodes each carries, and whether the run''s pressure range stays inside them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene''s answers are: the oil is the field''s designed material balance fluid, the gas is a standard correlation on the field''s gas gravity, and the tank model, flood ledger and sweep analysis all used the oil. Three sentences in a comment and the next person does not reconstruct it.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 11, 'Ekene''s gas table is correlated while its oil is designed. Why the difference?', '["Gas properties are far less sensitive to the choice of correlation, so a correlated gas description introduces negligible uncertainty into anything the study goes on to compute.", "The gas table is only used below the bubble point, which Ekene never reaches, so its provenance does not matter.", "No earlier course built a gas description, so there was nothing to inherit and inventing one would have meant designing a fluid nobody would check.", "Designing a gas table requires a compositional analysis, which the field''s fluid sampling programme never included."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The material balance course modelled Ekene as undersaturated with no free gas, the flood ledger set the gas factor to zero, and the SCAL course worked in oil and water. A correlation on the field''s own gas gravity is the honest fallback, derived from a real property and making no claim to be a measurement.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 12, 'Why does an undersaturated reservoir still need a gas table?', '["Because the equilibration requires a gas density to compute the pressure gradient above the gas-oil contact.", "Because the solution gas ratio in PVTO is interpolated against the gas table''s own pressure nodes during initialisation.", "Because the simulator computes a three-phase relative permeability whether or not free gas is present in any cell.", "Because near-wellbore pressures fall well below the average, and because a forecast can go anywhere."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A producer pulling against a floor of 1200 psia has cells around it well below the 2000 psia bubble point even while the field average sits above it. A deck omitting the gas table for an undersaturated oil cannot model its own producers'' drawdown.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 13, 'What one-line check confirms the gas table and the DENSITY block describe the same gas?', '["Divide the gas surface density by the density of air and confirm it matches the gravity the PVT was generated at.", "Compare the gas formation volume factor at the initial pressure against the ideal gas value at the same conditions.", "Confirm the gas viscosity at the lowest table pressure lies between 0.01 and 0.02 cp, which is the range for light gases.", "Check that the gas table''s pressure range brackets the bubble point, since that is where free gas first appears."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s gas density of 0.0572715 lb/ft3 is 0.75 times air, and the table was generated at 0.75. Those two agreeing is not automatic: they come from different keywords and nothing checks them against each other.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 14, 'Is mixing a designed oil with a correlated gas inconsistent?', '["Yes, because a single PROPS section must draw all its fluid descriptions from one source to be internally coherent.", "Only if the two describe fluids that could not coexist, and here they do not interact at all in the field''s history.", "Yes, because the solution gas in the oil table and the free gas in the gas table are the same molecules at different pressures.", "No, because provenance is a documentation concern rather than a physical one and the simulator cannot tell the difference."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oil''s dissolved gas is described entirely by the oil table''s solution gas ratio, and the gas table describes free gas, which this reservoir does not have in its history. What WOULD be inconsistent is a gas gravity of 0.75 in one keyword and 0.65 in another.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fluid-provenance', 15, 'A colleague argues that a correlated PVT is more defensible because correlations are objective. What is the answer?', '["Correlations are objective but imprecise, so a designed value is preferable whenever the study can tolerate the uncertainty.", "Correlations are objective only when their validity ranges are respected, and Ekene''s gravities fall outside Standing''s range.", "A correlation is a fit to somebody else''s fluids, so using it asserts that yours resembles that population, which is an assumption rather than an observation.", "Correlations are objective and the argument is right, which is why the deck''s gas table uses one rather than a designed set."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On a fluid you have designed or measured, that assumption is worse than the design or the measurement. Correlations are what you use when you have gravities and nothing else, which is exactly why this deck''s gas table uses one.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 1, 'Six Corey parameters became 22 SWOF rows. What is lost in the conversion?', '["The endpoint values, which are implied by the first and last rows rather than being carried explicitly in the table.", "The parameters themselves: a reader can see the curve but cannot tell it is a Corey curve or recover the exponents without refitting.", "The capillary pressure, which the Corey model describes but which the tabulated form has no column to express.", "Nothing at all, since a table evaluated on a fine enough grid carries exactly the same information as the analytic model that was used to produce it in the first place."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fix is a comment naming the model and its parameters, because a deck whose SWOF block has one is auditable and one without it is 22 numbers. The endpoints ARE the first and last rows, and the format does carry a Pc column, which this deck fills with zeros.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 2, 'SWOF''s second row is Sw = 0.37 with krw = 0.0001677050983124846. What does that number say?', '["The measurement resolution of the laboratory apparatus was about 1e-4, so any smaller mobility was recorded as this floor value rather than as a true zero.", "Water becomes mobile immediately above connate saturation, which is why the value is non-zero rather than exactly zero there.", "Two percent along the mobile range, water has about one two-thousandth of its endpoint mobility, because the water exponent is 2.5.", "The value is an interpolation artifact between the first two tabulated rows and carries no physical meaning of its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Normalise: (0.37 - 0.35)/(1 - 0.35 - 0.25) = 0.05, and 0.3 times 0.05 to the power 2.5 gives that number exactly. A water exponent of 2.5 makes water very reluctant to move at low saturation, and that reluctance is a large part of why displacement works at all.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 3, 'Why does the deck carry a table rather than the Corey model itself?', '["Because the format has no way to express a saturation model: every simulator interpolates between rows, and there is no keyword for Corey with these exponents.", "Because a table is faster to evaluate at run time than the power law the model would require at every timestep.", "Because different simulators implement Corey differently, so a table is the only portable way to state the same curve.", "Because the model was fitted to laboratory points and the table is the points themselves rather than the fit through them."]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is a deliberate design in the file format: it keeps the simulator independent of any particular saturation model, at the cost of losing the provenance at the boundary. The same trade appears in PROPS generally, which carries numbers rather than correlations.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 4, 'Which four numbers set the scale of the displacement, as opposed to its shape?', '["The two Corey exponents and the two endpoint mobilities, since exponents control curvature and endpoints control magnitude.", "The first and last rows of each of the two tables, which together bracket the range every other row is interpolated within.", "The connate water, the residual oil, the row count and the row spacing, which set both the range and its resolution.", "Connate water, residual oil, and the two endpoint mobilities."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Connate water sets the oil in place, residual oil sets the maximum displacement efficiency, and the two endpoint mobilities set the mobility ratio. Everything between the endpoints is shape, which is what the exponents control.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 5, 'Why do endpoint errors hide more easily than exponent errors?', '["Because endpoints are quoted to fewer significant figures than exponents, so a small error rounds away invisibly.", "Because two curves with the same exponents and different residual oil look nearly identical over most of their range and differ only in where they stop.", "Because endpoints appear only in the first and last rows, which most plotting packages omit from a rendered curve.", "Because endpoints are checked by the validator while exponents are not, so a reviewer assumes any error is in the exponent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Plot two tables with different exponents and the difference is a visible bulge; plot two with different residual oil and the eye stops looking exactly where they differ. Yet a residual oil of 0.30 rather than 0.25 removes an eighth of the movable oil from the field.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 6, 'Beyond the axis-closure check, what second endpoint check should a pair of tables pass?', '["The two tables'' row counts must match, since both are evaluated on the same normalised saturation grid.", "The water endpoint in SWOF and the gas endpoint in SGOF must sum to one, mirroring the relationship the two saturation axes already have with each other.", "SWOF''s oil mobility at connate water and SGOF''s oil mobility at zero gas must agree, because both describe oil as the only mobile phase.", "The capillary pressure at the last row of each table must be zero, which is the condition at the free water level."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A deck where those differ describes a rock whose oil permeability depends on which other phase is absent, which is not a thing. Ekene''s tables agree at 0.9 in both. Row counts differ legitimately at 22 and 21, and the endpoint mobilities have no reason to sum to anything.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 7, 'Where did Ekene''s gas-oil table come from?', '["Three design constants chosen for this deck, because the field never goes below its bubble point so no measurement constrains a gas-oil curve.", "The same SCAL fit that produced the water-oil table, evaluated on the gas saturation axis rather than the water one.", "A correlation on the oil gravity and the gas gravity, in the same way the gas PVT table was generated for this deck.", "The published SPE1 gas-oil table, which is the standard fallback when a field has no gas-oil measurements of its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The SCAL course did what a good course does with a curve nobody can constrain: it did not invent one. The deck has to carry one anyway, so the choice is between designing one and saying so, or leaving a default in place and not noticing.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 8, 'How should a designed table be treated?', '["Replace it with a correlation as soon as one is available, since a correlated table is better constrained than a designed one.", "Exclude it from the deck until a measurement exists, since a designed table asserts knowledge the study does not have.", "Report it alongside its uncertainty range, since a designed value without a range is indistinguishable from a measured one.", "Label it, grade nothing on it, and test whether the answer is sensitive to it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is the same treatment the waterflood course gave to enhanced recovery screening: taught, never graded. If a forecast turns out to be sensitive to a curve nobody measured, the study has a problem and the right response is a sensitivity case rather than a tighter number. The deck cannot omit the table.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 9, 'Ekene''s critical gas saturation is 0.05. What does it decide, and why does it barely matter here?', '["The saturation at which the gas-oil table begins, which is why SGOF''s first row sits at 0.05 rather than at zero.", "The gas saturation at which the oil becomes immobile, which sets the maximum recovery a gas drive can achieve.", "How much gas the reservoir retains before it can flow, which matters in a solution gas drive and barely bites on a field that stays undersaturated.", "The threshold below which the simulator treats gas as dissolved rather than free, which is a numerical convenience of the solver rather than a physical property of the rock at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Below it gas is present and immobile. That parameter has a large effect in a solution gas drive because it decides how much energy the reservoir keeps, and it could be chosen without argument here precisely because Ekene stays above its bubble point. SGOF''s first row is at Sg of zero.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 10, 'One saturation region for 4500 cells across five layers spanning a factor of six in permeability. What does that assume?', '["That the layers are in capillary equilibrium with each other, so a single averaged curve describes their combined behaviour adequately for a screening-scale model.", "That pore structure, and therefore the shape of the relative permeability curves, is the same in the 607 md layer as in the 102 md one.", "That the flood sweeps the layers simultaneously, so their individual curves would be averaged in any case.", "That the simulator scales the curves internally by each cell''s permeability, which is why one region suffices."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is usually wrong in detail, because pore structure is what makes one layer 600 md and another 100. A high-permeability layer typically has a lower connate water saturation and a different shape. Simulators support multiple regions for exactly this reason, and this deck uses one because the SCAL course fitted one curve.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 11, 'The deck has no hysteresis. Where would that matter?', '["Wherever saturation reverses: near an injector that is shut in, and around a rising contact.", "Everywhere, since drainage and imbibition differ at every saturation and the deck can only carry one of them.", "Nowhere in a waterflood, since water saturation rises monotonically in every cell throughout the displacement.", "Only below the bubble point, where gas coming out of solution reverses the oil saturation path in every cell."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Modelling hysteresis needs a second set of curves and a switching rule, and this deck has neither. That is fine for a monotonic flood and would not be for a cycled gas storage project. Saturation does not rise monotonically everywhere: a shut-in injector''s neighbourhood is the standard counterexample.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 12, 'When oil, water and gas are all present, where does the oil relative permeability come from?', '["A third table, SOF3, which a deck must supply whenever all three phases can be mobile in the same cell.", "The product of the two tabulated oil curves, which is the standard and unambiguous combination in the format.", "The water-oil table alone, since the oil-gas interaction is already captured by the gas relative permeability curve.", "A three-phase model combining the two tables, chosen as a simulator setting rather than supplied by the deck."]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are several three-phase models in common use and they give visibly different answers, so it is one of the least-examined choices in a study. On an undersaturated field it never bites; on a gas cap expansion it can dominate.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 13, 'Relative permeability in this format is a function of saturation alone. Which process breaks that?', '["A waterflood at high rate, where viscous forces overcome capillary forces and the endpoints shift measurably.", "A depletion below the bubble point, where the appearance of a third phase changes the two-phase curves themselves.", "A surfactant flood, where interfacial tension changes and the curves move with it.", "A thermal process, where temperature changes viscosity but leaves the relative permeability curves unaffected."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is precisely why enhanced recovery processes need different tables and often a different simulator. Rate, pressure and temperature effects on relative permeability are genuinely negligible in a conventional waterflood, which is what makes the saturation-only form defensible here.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 14, 'What five questions should a reader ask of any PROPS section they inherit?', '["Which laboratory, which core, which plugs, which fluids, and whether the measurements were made at reservoir conditions.", "How many saturation regions, is there hysteresis, is capillary pressure present, what is the three-phase model, and where did each table come from.", "How many rows, what spacing, what range, what interpolation and whether the range covers the run''s expected saturations.", "Which simulator, which version, which unit system, which defaults, and whether any of the keywords has quietly changed meaning between one version and the next."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene''s answers are one, no, no, not applicable, and mixed provenance. All defensible, all worth writing down. The laboratory and tabulation questions matter too and they do not tell you what the tables ASSUME.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-rock-curves-from-the-lab', 15, 'A colleague says the tables describe the rock. What is the narrower and correct claim?', '["They describe a rock, under one saturation history, with no capillary pressure, at whatever conditions the underlying measurements were made.", "They describe the average rock of the reservoir, which is what a single saturation region is intended to represent.", "They describe the rock as it behaves at the wells, which is where the core was taken and where the curves were measured.", "They describe the rock''s flow behaviour but not its storage, which is carried separately by the porosity field in GRID."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gap between the two claims is where most relative permeability surprises live. On Ekene there is a further step: the curves came from a Corey fit, behind that were laboratory points, and behind those a core plug that may or may not represent the sand.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 1, 'With cells 100 m square and the deck''s origin half a cell south-west of the map origin, what cell does a well at (600, 1900) fall in?', '["(6, 19), because the indices are computed by dividing the coordinates by the cell size and taking the integer part directly.", "(7, 19), because the northing of 1900 falls exactly on a cell boundary and the tie-break assigns it to the lower index.", "(7, 20).", "(8, 21), because the half-cell offset shifts both indices up by one relative to the unshifted map coordinates."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Add 50, divide by 100, take the floor, add one: 650/100 floors to 6 and gives 7, and 1950/100 floors to 19 and gives 20. The offset is what makes a well on the 100 m lattice land on a centre rather than a boundary, so no tie-break is needed.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 2, 'Which three mistakes does posting the wells on the model''s own structure map catch?', '["An off-by-one in the indices, a mismatched origin, and a flipped axis convention.", "A wrong variogram range, a wrong regional mean, and a wrong nugget, all of which move the surface at the wells.", "A wrong cell size, a wrong layer count, and a wrong net-to-gross, all of which change the depths the model reports.", "A wrong contact, a wrong porosity, and a wrong formation volume factor, which together set the model''s oil in place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is the only check that catches an axis flip, because every numerical summary of a flipped grid is identical to the correct one. The kriging parameters do not move the surface AT the wells, since a zero nugget makes it exact there.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 3, 'Ekene''s producers are completed through all five layers. What does that assert, and is it consistent with the field''s own analysis?', '["That the well was perforated over the whole gross interval, which the completion report for each producer confirms.", "That every layer can flow to the well, and yes: it is exactly the situation the waterflood course''s layered sweep described.", "That the layers are in pressure communication, which a fully completed well would force even if the rock did not.", "That the fastest layer dominates production, which is a modelling assumption rather than something the completion interval itself asserts about the well."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 607 md layer breaks through first while the 102 md layer is still mostly unswept, and the producer sees the combination. Consistency between a deck''s completion and the analysis it came from is not automatic and is worth checking whenever a deck arrives with one attached.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 4, 'The five connections are not five equal contributions. What weights them?', '["The layer thickness alone, since the wellbore length in each layer is what the connection''s transmissibility is proportional to.", "The distance from the cell centre to the wellbore, which differs between layers because the well is not exactly central.", "The pressure difference between each layer and the wellbore, which the simulator recomputes at every timestep of the run.", "The well index, which scales roughly with each layer''s permeability-thickness."]'::jsonb, 3, 'So a connection in the 607 md layer delivers far more than one in the 102 md layer, and the producer''s output is dominated by layer 2. That is how the waterflood course''s conclusion that the fast layer carries the flood follows in the deck as well.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 5, 'The wellbore radius of 0.35 ft enters the well index logarithmically. What follows?', '["It is one of the least sensitive inputs in the deck, so a wrong radius is not the explanation for a well that will not match.", "It is one of the most sensitive inputs, because a logarithm amplifies small relative changes in the radius into large absolute changes in the index.", "It cancels entirely for a vertical well, which is why the same value is used for all seven wells in this deck.", "It must match the drilled hole size exactly, because the simulator uses it to compute the wellbore storage volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Doubling it changes the well index by a few percent. That insensitivity is worth knowing because people spend time on it anyway. There is no wellbore storage in this formulation, since the simulator does not model a wellbore.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 6, 'A well is perforated over 2 ft at the top of a 7 ft layer. How does the deck represent that?', '["As a fractional connection, with the well index scaled by the perforated fraction of the layer thickness.", "As a connection with a skin factor computed from the partial penetration, which is the standard correction applied whenever a well is open over only part of a layer''s thickness.", "As one connection, identical to a well perforated over the full 7 ft, because a completion is as resolved as the grid.", "It cannot be represented, so the deck must either open the whole layer or leave it shut entirely."]'::jsonb, 2, 'If that distinction matters the model needs more layers. Skin and partial-penetration corrections do exist as COMPDAT fields and they are a way of representing the EFFECT rather than the geometry, which is a different thing from the grid resolving it.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 7, 'Ekene-1 produced 998.5247575266284 stb in January 2023. What rate does the schedule carry?', '["32.804 stb/d, from dividing by the mean month of 30.4375 days that the prediction tail also uses for consistency.", "32.210476049246076 stb/d, from dividing by January''s 31 days.", "998.5247575266284 stb/d, because WCONHIST carries volumes rather than rates when the history is monthly.", "31.79 stb/d, from dividing by 31.4 days, which is the average length of the months in the calendar year 2023."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conversion is one division by the month''s own length. Using a mean month instead gives 32.804 stb/d, which the simulator then applies over the 31 days the DATES block spans, producing 1.8 percent too much oil in that month alone.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 8, 'Why do the errors from a mean-month divisor not cancel over three years?', '["Because the errors compound, since each month''s error shifts the starting cumulative for the next month''s calculation.", "Because production declines through the history, so later months carry more weight in the total than earlier ones do.", "Because leap years introduce an additional day that the mean-month divisor of 30.4375 already accounts for twice over.", "Because they follow the calendar rather than anything random: every 31-day month is over-produced and every 30-day month under-produced."]'::jsonb, 3, 'February is over-produced by nearly ten percent. That is a distinctive symptom: a history match whose residuals correlate with month length has this bug and nothing else. The errors are systematic rather than compounding.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 9, 'What is the round-trip check on a history schedule?', '["Multiply each rate by the days its period spans, sum over wells and periods, and confirm the total reproduces the ledger''s own total.", "Compare the schedule''s first and last dates against the ledger''s, which confirms the periods span the same interval.", "Confirm the number of WCONHIST blocks equals the number of ledger months, which catches a dropped or duplicated period.", "Check that each well appears in every period, which catches the keyword persistence that would otherwise repeat a rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It catches every divisor mistake at once and it costs one line. The count checks and the persistence check are both worth doing and neither would catch a wrong divisor, because the counts would be right and the rates wrong.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 10, 'Ekene''s schedule carries no bottom-hole pressures. What does that limit?', '["The model cannot be run, because a well needs either a rate or a pressure to be controlled on at every period.", "The model''s initialisation, which needs a measured pressure at the datum depth and has to fall back on the tank model''s own estimate instead.", "The model''s pressures are unconstrained by observation, so a rate-controlled match tests the model only through the pressures it predicts.", "The model''s forecast, because the prediction period inherits the last observed bottom-hole pressure as its starting control."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is normal and worth stating rather than discovering. The history carries rates, so the model reproduces rates by construction, and everything it must predict is where the evidence is. Initialisation uses the EQUIL datum pressure, which is supplied.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 11, 'Both Ekene injectors found the top of the sand below the 1560 m contact. What does that do in the model?', '["Their cells are excluded from the oil in place, which is why the deck''s oil cell count is lower than the booking''s.", "Their cells initialise at a water saturation of one, so they are injecting water into water and support the oil indirectly.", "Their completions must be restricted to the layers above the contact, or the simulator will refuse the well at initialisation.", "Their injection is booked as out of zone, which is where the waterflood course''s twelve percent figure comes from."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oil is up-structure, and the injection supports it by pressure and by displacing water toward it rather than by contacting it directly. That is the two-jobs distinction from the waterflood course, falling out of the deck''s geometry rather than being asserted separately.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 12, 'Why is completing a water-leg injector through the whole column defensible?', '["It is not defensible, and the deck does it only because the emitter has no way to express a selective completion for an injector rather than for a producer.", "It maximises the pressure support delivered per barrel, because a longer completion contacts more of the aquifer volume.", "It matches the producers'' completions, and the model requires injectors and producers to be completed identically.", "The whole column is water-bearing, so every layer is a valid target and spreading the injection keeps the injection pressure down."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The alternative, completing only the upper layers closer to the contact, is a real design argument on the theory that water injected higher up displaces oil sooner, and the model can test it. That is one of the things a simulation model is for.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 13, 'What two things should a reader check on any deck with water-leg injectors?', '["The injectivity against the pressure ceiling, and whether the injected water moves down-dip into the aquifer rather than up toward the oil.", "The completion interval and the perforation density, which together set how much of the water leg is actually contacted.", "The water salinity and the injection temperature, which decide whether scale or fines migration will damage the injectors.", "The distance to the nearest producer and the allocation factor, which together decide how much of the injected water each of the producers actually ends up receiving."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s injectors carry a ceiling of 4500 psia. Water injected below a contact can move down-dip rather than up-dip, and the waterflood course booked twelve percent of the field''s injection as out of zone on exactly that reasoning, which a simulation model can test.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 14, 'Why is a well name effectively permanent once a study has history?', '["Because the simulator writes the name into the restart file''s header, so a rename invalidates every saved restart.", "Because the eight-character limit means most short names are already taken, so a rename usually forces a scheme change.", "Because every per-well output vector is keyed by name, so the name is the join between the deck, the results and every plot built from them.", "Because well names are validated against the operator''s licence records once a study has been formally submitted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A rename mid-study breaks the join with every previous run, so it is a data migration rather than a cosmetic edit. That is why the naming decision is a five-minute job at the start of a study that nobody wants to make and everybody should.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-wells-on-a-real-field', 15, 'Before building a deck, what list is worth ten minutes?', '["Every keyword the deck will use, checked against the target simulator''s manual for version-specific behaviour.", "Every well the study will ever include, including side-tracks, re-entries and planned wells, checked for uniqueness in the first eight characters.", "Every table the PROPS section will need, with the source and date of each recorded before any of them is written.", "Every cell property the grid will carry, with the averaging rule for each stated before the property model is upscaled."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is the only way to catch a collision that would otherwise appear as a well quietly missing from the results, because a simulator seeing a second declaration for an existing name treats it as a redefinition rather than an error.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the deck''s STOIIP. What is the likely mistake?', '["Using the booking''s porosity of 0.2 rather than the deck''s own, which differ because the deck carries net rather than gross thickness.", "Clipping the column at the contact rather than by cell centre, which gives a larger number because a column just above the contact then contributes a partial layer.", "Summing over all 900 columns rather than only the ones with oil, which inflates the answer by the ratio of the two counts.", "Applying the formation volume factor twice, once in the hydrocarbon pore volume and again in the conversion to stock tank barrels."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The check is that your answer should sit within a tenth of a percent of the booking, because the model was calibrated to be. The porosity is 0.2 in both, and a column with no oil contributes zero to the sum whether or not you include it.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'Capstone field 2 asks for the gap against the booking. Which sign, and what is the likely slip?', '["Negative, because the deck is the smaller; the slip is computing it as a fraction of the deck''s volume rather than the booking''s.", "Positive, because the deck''s larger oil area more than compensates for its thinner average column.", "Negative, and the slip is using the column-clipped volume, which would give a positive answer instead.", "Either, depending on the convention, which is why the field states the clipping rule explicitly in its prompt."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two denominators differ in the fourth decimal place of a small number, which is inside the tolerance for one and outside for the other. The column-clipped convention is a real alternative and it is a different field rather than this one''s likely slip.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'Capstone field 3 asks for the oil cell count. What are the two mistakes?', '["Counting only the columns with a full five layers of oil, and counting the columns whose top lies above the contact regardless of how many of their layers qualify.", "Counting the booking''s 169 rather than the deck''s, and counting the cells in the water leg as well as those in the oil leg.", "Counting cells rather than columns, which is several times larger, and using the column-clipped rule, which changes which columns qualify.", "Counting the columns the wells fall in, and counting the columns within one variogram range of a well rather than all of them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The check is that the answer must exceed the booking''s 169, because matching the volume over a thinner average column requires more area. A column whose top is a foot above the contact contributes oil under one rule and not the other.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'A learner submits 1565 m as the depth the model carries at Ekene-2. What have they read?', '["Reporting the depth of the layer base rather than the layer top, which differs by one layer thickness at that column.", "Reporting the depth in feet rather than in metres, which is the unit the deck itself stores its depths in and what the TOPS block therefore carries throughout.", "Reporting the kriged value at the well''s exact position, which is 1565 m and is what an exact interpolator must return there.", "Reporting the mapped top of 1565 m, which is the well''s depth rather than the deck''s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The check is that the answer should be shallower than 1565 and by less than a metre. If it equals 1565 exactly you have read the well database rather than the deck. Reading the kriged value at the well''s own position is the same mistake seen from the other side, and it is the reason it happens.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 5 asks for the correlated formation volume factor at initial pressure. What is the likely mistake?', '["Reading the designed value of 1.2 instead of the correlated one, which is the whole point the field is testing.", "Reading the value at the bubble point instead, which is larger, so the answer is wrong in a direction that looks right.", "Evaluating the correlation at surface temperature rather than at the stated reservoir temperature of 180 degrees Fahrenheit.", "Using the wrong correlation, since several are available and the deck does not state which one its gas table used."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Above the bubble point Bo decreases with pressure, so the bubble point value is the larger one. Confusing the designed and correlated values is a possible error and it is a much more obvious one; the temperature and the correlation set are both stated in the prompt.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'Capstone field 6 asks for the solution gas gap as a percentage. What is the likely mistake?', '["A unit slip: the deck carries solution gas in Mscf/stb so its top node reads 0.4, and comparing 421.94 against 0.4 gives nonsense.", "Comparing against the ledger''s 400 scf/stb rather than the designed value, which are the same number arrived at differently.", "Using the bubble point pressure rather than the initial pressure, which is where the correlated solution gas is evaluated.", "Reporting the absolute difference rather than the percentage, which would give an absolute gas ratio difference rather than the percentage the field actually asks for."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The check is that the answer is a few percent and the correlated value is the larger. The ledger''s 400 scf/stb IS the designed value, so comparing against it is correct rather than a mistake.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'What do all six Professional capstone fields have in common?', '["Each requires a unit conversion between the deck''s field units and the metric units the field was mapped in.", "Each is computed from the deck alone, so the booking and the well database are context rather than inputs.", "Each holds two sources against each other, so before submitting you should name the two and which one the question asked for.", "Each depends on the calibration, which is why the prompt states the regional mean explicitly rather than leaving it to be derived."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The deck against the booking, the deck against the well database, the design against the correlation. Only one of the six involves a unit conversion, four of them need a source outside the deck, and the calibration parameter belongs to the Expert tier.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'The tier''s summary says the structure is calibrated rather than validated. What would a validation have needed?', '["A formal review of the calibration by a second engineer, with the acceptance criterion agreed before the tuning began.", "A second volumetric booking computed independently on the deck''s own frame, so the two could be compared like with like.", "A sensitivity run at a shallower and a deeper regional mean, showing the booked volume falls inside the resulting spread.", "Something the model was not tuned to reproduce, such as a seventh well whose top the surface then predicts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene has none spare: all six wells went into the interpolation and the field has no independent structural data. That is the honest closing line, and it tells the next person exactly what to go and get. The sensitivity run is worth doing and it tests nothing the model was not already tuned against.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'Which of the tier''s three caveats concerns precision?', '["The deck carries fifteen digits while the underlying production history is an allocation good to a few percent.", "The structure is calibrated to the booking, so the agreement is arranged rather than independently arrived at.", "One saturation region asserts that a 607 md layer and a 102 md layer flow alike, which is not what rock does.", "The gas-oil table has no measurement behind it at all, so nothing in the course is graded on its three parameters."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Precision is not accuracy. A monthly per-well oil volume is typically good to a few percent, and the deck carries it to fifteen figures because that is what a double holds rather than what the measurement supports. The other three are real caveats about different things.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'The Expert tier is described as moving from auditing to authoring. What does that change?', '["The tier stops reading the deck and starts reading the results, which is where a study''s conclusions actually live.", "Every decision now has to be made and defended rather than found and checked, which is why its central lesson is about reporting.", "The tier moves from the static model to the dynamic one, so the questions asked of the deck become about flow behaviour rather than about geometry and provenance.", "The tier begins running the deck, which is the step this course has deliberately deferred until the last module."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A trajectory has to become a completion list, a database has to become a schedule, and a free parameter has to be tuned and the tuning reported. The Expert tier does have a results module and it grades none of it, and the course never runs a deck.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'What two things from this tier does the Expert tier assume?', '["The kriging parameters and the well positions, since the Expert tier re-intersects trajectories against the same grid.", "The PVT provenance and the rock curve provenance, since the Expert calibration adjusts both to match the booking.", "The reconciliation and the fact that it was deliberately arranged, and the cell-centre rule for assigning a cell to oil or to water.", "The eight-character naming rule and the completion strategy, since the Expert tier adds a well to the existing model."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Its calibration module picks the reconciliation up directly and expects you to know what was matched and what it cost. Every volumetric statement in the next tier depends on knowing that Eclipse decides a cell''s fluid by its centre depth, and that the alternative gives a different and equally defensible number.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'What does the tier say about the deck''s 900 column depths and its six measurements?', '["That the six anchors are exact and the rest carry a kriging variance that the deck records alongside each depth.", "That the interpolated depths should be excluded from the volumetric calculation as insufficiently constrained by data.", "That the ratio is unusually poor for a developed field, which is why the calibration was necessary in the first place.", "That 894 of them are interpolated, so the structure of the model is a hypothesis with six anchors."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is the normal condition of every reservoir model ever built and the point of saying it out loud. The deck carries no uncertainty at all, no cell is excluded from the volumetrics, and six wells over this area is entirely ordinary.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'The tier reports the deck''s fluid divergence from the correlation as a taught result rather than a seeded one. What does that mean in practice?', '["The correlated values appear in the lessons and the deck carries the designed ones, so a learner sees the gap without the model inheriting it.", "The correlated values are carried in the deck as a commented-out alternative, so a reader can substitute them and re-run.", "The correlated values are used for the gas table and the designed ones for the oil, which is how the two provenances are mixed.", "The correlated values are graded at the Expert tier rather than here, so the Professional tier states them without testing them."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gas table being correlated is a separate and true fact about the deck. The divergence itself is measured in the lessons and two of its numbers are graded at this tier, which is what makes it taught rather than merely mentioned.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'What is the four-line provenance note the tier asks you to write?', '["One line each for the grid, the fluids, the wells and the schedule, matching the four sections that carry data.", "One line each for the structure, the volume, the oil and the rock curves, saying where each came from.", "One line each for the author, the date, the simulator version and the study the deck belongs to, as a header block.", "One line each for the six wells, the booking, the SCAL fit and the ledger, naming the four external sources used."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene''s would read: structure kriged from six mapped tops with a calibrated regional mean, volume matched to the booking under the cell-centre convention, oil designed rather than correlated, rock curves one Corey fit applied everywhere.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'What does the tier say a deck read on its own always is?', '["Incomplete, because a deck cannot express the provenance of any of the numbers it carries.", "Ambiguous, because the same numbers can describe several different reservoirs depending on the conventions assumed.", "Self-consistent, because it is one file; the disagreements only appear when it is read against its sources.", "Sufficient, because everything a simulator needs is in it and nothing outside it changes what the model will do."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is why this tier''s whole method is comparison: against the mapping, the booking, the laboratory report and the production database. A deck IS sufficient for a simulator and that is exactly the problem, because sufficiency for a solver is not evidence about a field.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'Ekene has six wells and 900 grid columns. What does that ratio say about the model''s structure?', '["That the model is under-constrained and its structure should not be used for volumetrics until more wells are drilled.", "That the six wells were placed to sample the structure evenly, which is why the interpolation between them is reliable.", "That 894 of the 900 column depths are interpolated, so the structure of the model is a hypothesis carrying only six anchors.", "That the interpolation carries an estimation variance the deck records alongside each column, which is how the structural uncertainty is propagated forward."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is the normal condition of every reservoir model ever built, and saying it out loud is the point. The deck carries no uncertainty at all, and the wells were placed by drilling history rather than by a sampling design.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'Which three things must an interpolator decide that the data does not determine?', '["How far a well''s influence reaches, what the surface does far from any well, and whether it passes exactly through the data.", "The cell size, the number of layers, and the depth reference the finished surface is quoted against in the deck.", "Which of the wells to include, how heavily to weight the measurement error carried by each, and where to place the grid origin relative to the map.", "Whether to grid in time or depth, which velocity model to use, and how to tie the converted surface back to the wells."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Range, regional behaviour and exactness at the data. None is determined by six depths and each visibly changes the map. Cell size and layering are gridding decisions downstream of the surface.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'What does a kriging nugget of zero make the estimator?', '["An unbiased estimator, meaning its errors average to zero across the whole grid even though individual cells differ.", "A smoother, because the covariance at short separation is maximised and neighbouring estimates are pulled together.", "Numerically unstable, because a zero nugget makes the covariance matrix singular when two data points fall close together.", "An exact interpolator at the data points."]'::jsonb, 3, 'This deck was built to demonstrate it, which makes it a check on the whole chain from well database to TOPS block. A non-zero nugget is what you want when the measurements carry error.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'Five Ekene wells are recovered exactly and one is not. What separates them?', '["The sixth was withheld from the interpolation as a blind test of the resulting surface''s predictive accuracy.", "The five sit at coordinates that are multiples of the 100 m cell, so with the deck''s half-cell offset they land on cell centres.", "The sixth was logged with a different tool, so its recorded top carries a systematic depth shift the surface cannot honour.", "The five lie within one variogram range of a neighbouring well, so their estimates are supported by other measurements as well as by themselves."]'::jsonb, 1, 'All six went into the interpolation, so none is a blind test and the field has no spare well for one. Ekene-2 at a northing of 1150 m has no cell centre at its position, so it inherits the depth of the nearest one.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'Two of the exactly-recovered wells carry a residual of about 2.3e-13 m. What is that?', '["The metre to foot and back round trip, because the deck stores depths in feet and the conversion is not exact in binary.", "The convergence tolerance of the kriging solve, which stops when successive estimates differ by less than that amount.", "A genuine structural residual, since simple kriging is only exact in the limit and this nugget is small rather than zero.", "The precision of the coordinate transform between the map frame and the deck frame, applied twice in opposite directions."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two tenths of a picometre is arithmetic rather than structure. A discrepancy at the thirteenth decimal is floating point and one at the second is a modelling decision, and knowing which you are looking at is the skill.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Ekene-2 lands 0.68 m shallower than its log. Whose limitation is that?', '["The interpolator''s, because kriging cannot honour a point that falls between the nodes of its estimation grid.", "The variogram''s, because a 1200 m range cannot resolve a 50 m lateral offset and a shorter range would remove it.", "The grid''s, because the deck samples the surface only at cell centres and no centre lies at that well''s position.", "The well database''s, because the surveyed surface position carries a lateral uncertainty of about half a cell at this depth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Kriging did pass exactly through the well; the surface is correct there. This is a gridding loss rather than an interpolation loss, and the distinction matters because a finer grid reduces it while a better interpolator does not.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'What bounds a grid''s positional error in general?', '["The variogram range, since a point is moved to the nearest centre within its own zone of influence.", "Half a cell in each direction times the local gradient, which can be computed before anything is built.", "One cell in each direction, since a point on a boundary may be assigned to either of the cells sharing it.", "Nothing in general, because a point outside the grid entirely is moved to the nearest cell however far away it is."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That bound applies to wells, seismic picks, core plugs and pressure gauges alike. A point outside the grid is not moved at all: it returns no cell, which is a different failure mode.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'The deck''s volume and the booking were computed by the same engine on the same contact, porosity and saturation. What does that buy?', '["A guarantee of agreement, since the engine is deterministic and both runs were given identical inputs.", "An independent check on the deck, because two runs of a validated engine agreeing confirms the model is correct.", "A saving in effort, since the booking''s answer can be reused directly rather than recomputed on the deck''s own frame.", "Every remaining difference has to be geometry or clipping convention, which is what makes the residual between them attributable at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hold constant everything you can, so the residual is attributable. The inputs are not identical: the surfaces and the frames differ, which is exactly the point of the comparison.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Why is a gap of six hundredths of a percent between two independently built models suspicious?', '["Because two independently built models do not agree to that many figures by accident, so a reader is entitled to ask what made it happen.", "Because a simulation model should hold less oil than a volumetric estimate, so close agreement suggests a clipping error.", "Because the volumetric booking carries only three significant figures of genuine precision, so an agreement quoted at five figures is meaningless anyway.", "Because the frames differ, so exact agreement would require the deck''s cells to align with the booking''s cell by cell."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It was arranged: the structure carries one free parameter and it was set so this number would come out. Doing that deliberately and saying so is the honest version; presenting it as independent confirmation is not.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'How does the Eclipse cell-centre rule decide a column''s oil contribution?', '["On the column''s top: if the top is above the contact the whole column is oil and otherwise none of it is.", "By the fraction of the column above the contact, applied uniformly across all five of its layers at once.", "Layer by layer, on each individual layer''s own centre depth, so a column contributes only whole layers of oil and never a partial one.", "From the capillary pressure curve, which assigns each layer a saturation from its height above the free water level."]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no partial cell, so the modelled oil volume is a staircase whose tread is a cell and whose riser is a layer thickness. The fractional rule is the tapered convention and the capillary rule is what a deck with a real Pc curve would do.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'The column-clipped convention gives a larger volume than the cell-centre one. By roughly how much, and why?', '["About eight percent, which is the fraction of columns the contact passes through on this particular structure.", "About 0.79 percent of the deck''s volume, because clipping the column follows the contact exactly instead of rounding it to layer boundaries.", "About 0.06 percent, which is the same size as the residual left over from the calibration against the booking.", "It gives a smaller volume, because clipping removes the parts of each layer that lie below the contact depth."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gap is a direct measure of vertical resolution at the contact: five layers over 34.6 ft means a tread of about 7 ft, and refining the layering makes the two conventions converge.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Which convention should be reported as the model''s oil in place, and why?', '["The cell-centre one, because that is the oil the simulator will actually be producing from once the deck is run on this grid.", "The column-clipped one, because it follows the contact exactly and is the more physically accurate of the two.", "The booking''s own convention, because that is what the field''s reserves are already stated against and consistency across the study matters more than anything.", "The average of the two, because the truth lies between them and neither is more defensible than the other."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Report the other alongside when the gap is material, because the difference tells a reader how resolution-limited the model is at the contact.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'What was tuned to make the deck''s volume match the booking?', '["The porosity, which was reduced slightly from the booking''s 0.2 to bring the deck''s pore volume into line.", "The contact depth, which was deepened within the uncertainty of the mapping until the two volumes agreed.", "The layer thicknesses, which were rescaled away from the waterflood column''s own proportions until the resulting net pay matched what the booking assumed.", "The kriging regional mean, which is the depth the interpolated surface reverts to once it is far away from any of the wells."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the least constrained thing in the static model: the wells are measurements, the contact is mapped, and the porosity and saturation came from earlier courses. Tuning any of those would override a measurement with a preference.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'The deck matches the booked volume over 266 oil cells where the booking used 169. What does that mismatch mean?', '["The model is optimistic, since more oil-bearing cells means more oil available to the wells at any given time.", "The model is on a finer frame, so its cell count is naturally larger and the two numbers are not comparable.", "The model''s oil is spread over a larger area at a thinner average column, which is the cost of matching volume rather than area.", "The model has lost vertical resolution, since spreading the same volume over a greater number of cells thins each one of them below a single layer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'One free parameter fixes one quantity. Optimism would be more VOLUME and the volume is matched; cell count describes distribution rather than magnitude, and layer thicknesses are unchanged.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Why does the Ekene deck carry a designed oil rather than a correlated one?', '["Because the designed values are closer to the laboratory measurements taken on an appraisal sample from Ekene-1.", "Because the tank pressure history, the flood ledger''s frozen factors and the fractional flow curves were all derived with the designed fluid.", "Because Standing''s correlation is not valid at 32 API and 180 F, which falls outside the range it was fitted over.", "Because a simulator cannot express a correlation in the PVTO record format, so a table of designed values is required."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Consistency across the study beats agreement with a correlation. There is no laboratory PVT for this field and the correlations are perfectly valid at these gravities.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'Standing gives Rs at the bubble point of 421.93922752270595 scf/stb against a designed 400. What is the gap?', '["About 5.5 percent, and read the other way it says an oil of this gravity carrying 400 scf/stb would bubble below 2000 psia.", "About 0.5 percent, which is inside the uncertainty of any correlation and therefore not a meaningful difference.", "About 22 scf/stb, which is an absolute difference rather than a percentage and is the form the field asks for.", "About 5.5 percent, which is the reason the deck carries the correlated value and records the designed one alongside it as the alternative."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The deck carries the DESIGNED value, and the divergence is taught rather than seeded. Both descriptions are defensible and the deck can carry only one.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'Using the correlated Bo of 1.2292846175634324 instead of 1.2 would do what to the oil in place?', '["Increase it by about 2.4 percent, since a larger factor means each reservoir barrel yields more stock tank oil.", "Leave it unchanged, because the hydrocarbon pore volume is computed before any formation volume factor is applied.", "Reduce it by about 2.4 percent, because the volumetric booking divides the reservoir oil volume by Bo to reach stock tank barrels.", "Change it unpredictably, because the formation volume factor enters both the numerator and the denominator of the volumetric expression at once."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is forty times the reconciliation residual the previous module worked to achieve. A larger Bo means each stock tank barrel occupied more reservoir space, so a fixed reservoir volume holds fewer of them.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'The correlated oil viscosity is 0.7341185203712621 cp against a designed 1.8. What would swapping it do to the flood?', '["Nothing, since relative permeability rather than viscosity sets the mobility in a simulation model.", "Improve the sweep slightly, within the uncertainty of the layered sweep calculation the waterflood course performed.", "Change only the displacement efficiency, since the areal and vertical sweeps depend on geometry rather than on fluids.", "Take the mobility ratio from 1.2 to about 0.49, turning a mildly unfavourable flood into a favourable one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A PVT substitution nobody would call major would invalidate an entire course''s worth of analysis. Mobility ratio drives the areal sweep correlation, the layered frontal positions and the fractional flow at once.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'What is the rule about a deck''s fluid?', '["It must be the most recently measured description available when the deck is built and run.", "It must be the same fluid description the rest of the study was matched against, whatever its provenance happens to be.", "It must come from a published correlation, so that anybody holding the two gravities can regenerate exactly the same fluid description independently.", "It must reproduce the observed bubble point, since that is the only PVT quantity measured directly in the field."]'::jsonb, 1, 'If a history match used one description and the deck carries another, the match does not transfer and the model reproduces history for the wrong reasons. That is not a tidiness rule.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'A new PVT report arrives and somebody updates the deck alone. What is the symptom months later?', '["A model that used to match and no longer does, with no change to the geology, until somebody finds the PVT commit.", "A deck that fails to validate, because the new tables disagree with the RSVD value in the SOLUTION section.", "An error raised at initialisation, since the oil in place the deck reports no longer matches the value carried in the study document.", "Results that differ between runs, because the two fluid descriptions are being mixed between report steps."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The deck runs perfectly and returns different answers. Volumes drift from the booking, the match stops reproducing pressures, and the ledger''s factors are stale, and none of it appears as an error.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'Why is Ekene''s gas table correlated while its oil is designed?', '["Gas properties are insensitive to the correlation chosen, so a correlated gas adds negligible uncertainty to anything.", "The gas table is only used below the bubble point, which Ekene never reaches, so its provenance does not matter.", "No earlier course built a gas description, so there was nothing to inherit and inventing one would mean designing a fluid nobody would check.", "Designing a gas table needs a compositional analysis, which the field''s fluid sampling programme never included."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The material balance course modelled Ekene as undersaturated, the ledger set the gas factor to zero and the SCAL course worked in oil and water. A correlation on the field''s own gas gravity is the honest fallback.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Six Corey parameters became 22 SWOF rows. What is lost?', '["The endpoints, which are implied by the first and last rows rather than being carried explicitly in the table.", "The capillary pressure, which the model describes and which the tabulated form has no column to express.", "Nothing, since a table evaluated on a fine enough grid carries the same information as the model behind it.", "The parameters themselves, and with them the name of the model, so a reader cannot tell it is a Corey curve at all without refitting one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fix is a comment naming the model and its parameters. The endpoints ARE the first and last rows, and the format does carry a Pc column, which this deck fills with zeros.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Why does the deck carry a table rather than the Corey model?', '["Because a table is faster to evaluate at run time than a power law would be at every timestep of the run.", "Because the format has no way to express a saturation model: every simulator interpolates rows and there is no keyword for Corey.", "Because simulators implement Corey differently, so a table is the only portable way to state the same curve.", "Because the model was fitted to laboratory points and the table is those points rather than the fit through them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is a deliberate design: it keeps the simulator independent of any particular saturation model, at the cost of losing the provenance at the boundary. The same trade appears throughout PROPS.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'Which four numbers set the scale of the displacement rather than its shape?', '["Connate water, residual oil and the two endpoint mobilities.", "The two Corey exponents and the two endpoint mobilities, since exponents give curvature and endpoints give magnitude.", "The first and last rows of each table, which bracket the range every interior row is interpolated within.", "Connate water, residual oil, the row count and the row spacing, which set both the range and its resolution."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Connate water sets the oil in place, residual oil sets the maximum displacement efficiency, and the endpoint mobilities set the mobility ratio. Everything between them is shape, which the exponents control.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'Two curves differ only in their residual oil saturation. Why is that hard to see?', '["Because endpoints are quoted to fewer significant figures, so a small error rounds away without being visible.", "Because endpoints appear only in the first and last rows, which most plotting packages omit when rendering a curve.", "Because two curves with the same exponents and different residual oil look nearly identical except where they stop, which is where the eye stops looking.", "Because the validator checks endpoints and not exponents, so a reviewer assumes any error must be in the exponent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A residual oil of 0.30 rather than 0.25 removes an eighth of the movable oil from the field, and the two curves look the same over most of their range.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'The SGOF table has three parameters behind it. What is their provenance?', '["The same SCAL fit as the water-oil table, evaluated on the gas saturation axis instead of the water one.", "Three design constants chosen for this deck, because the field never goes below its bubble point so nothing constrains a gas-oil curve.", "A correlation on the oil and gas gravities, in the same way the deck''s gas PVT table was generated.", "The published SPE1 gas-oil table, which is the standard fallback when a field has no gas-oil measurements."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The SCAL course did not invent a curve nobody could constrain. The deck must carry one anyway, so the choice is between designing one and saying so, or leaving a default in place and not noticing.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'A table nobody measured is in the deck. What is the correct treatment?', '["Replace it with a correlation as soon as one exists, since a correlated table is better constrained than a designed one.", "Leave it out of the deck until a measurement exists, since a designed table asserts knowledge the study does not have.", "Report it with an uncertainty range, since a designed value without one is indistinguishable from a measured value.", "Label it, grade nothing on it, and test whether the answer is sensitive to it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'That is the same treatment the waterflood course gave to enhanced recovery screening. If a forecast turns out sensitive to a curve nobody measured, the right response is a sensitivity case rather than a tighter number.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'One saturation region across five layers spanning a factor of six in permeability. What does that assume?', '["That pore structure, and so the shape of the relative permeability curves, is the same in the 607 md layer as in the 102 md one.", "That the five layers sit in capillary equilibrium with one another, so a single averaged curve describes their combined behaviour adequately.", "That the flood sweeps all five layers simultaneously, so their individual curves would be averaged in any case.", "That the simulator rescales the curves per cell by permeability, which is why a single region is sufficient here."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pore structure is what makes one layer 600 md and another 100. Simulators support multiple regions for exactly this reason, and this deck uses one because the SCAL course fitted one curve.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'A well sits at map coordinates (600, 1900) on a 100 m grid whose origin is half a cell south-west of the map origin. Which cell?', '["(6, 19), from dividing the coordinates by the cell size and taking the integer part without the offset.", "(7, 19), because a northing of 1900 falls on a boundary and the tie-break assigns it to the lower index.", "(7, 20).", "(8, 21), because the half-cell offset raises both indices by one relative to the unshifted map coordinates."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Add 50, divide by 100, floor, add one. The offset is what puts a well on the 100 m lattice at a cell centre rather than a boundary, so no tie-break arises.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'What does posting the wells on the model''s own structure map catch that no statistic does?', '["An axis flip, because every numerical summary of a flipped grid is identical to the correct one.", "A wrong variogram range, which moves the surface at the wells as well as between them.", "A wrong contact depth, which changes which columns carry oil and therefore the model''s volume.", "A wrong net-to-gross convention, which shifts every layer interface downward relative to the top of the column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It also catches an off-by-one and a mismatched origin. The kriging parameters do not move the surface AT the wells at all, because a zero nugget makes it exact there.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Ekene''s five connections per producer are not five equal contributions. What weights them?', '["The layer thickness alone, since the wellbore length in each layer sets the connection''s transmissibility.", "The well index of each connection, which scales roughly with the permeability-thickness product of the layer it sits in.", "The distance from each cell centre to the wellbore itself, which differs from layer to layer because the well does not pass exactly through the centre.", "The pressure drop between each layer and the wellbore, recomputed by the simulator at every timestep of the run."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A connection in the 607 md layer delivers far more than one in the 102 md layer, so the producer''s output is dominated by layer 2. That is how the waterflood course''s conclusion follows in the deck.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'January 2023 has 31 days and Ekene-1 made 998.5247575266284 stb in it. What goes in the schedule?', '["32.804 stb/d, from dividing by the mean month of 30.4375 days that the prediction tail also uses.", "998.5247575266284 stb/d, because WCONHIST carries monthly volumes directly when the history is monthly.", "31.79 stb/d, from dividing by 31.4 days, the average month length across the calendar year 2023.", "32.210476049246076 stb/d."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conversion is one division by January''s own 31 days. A mean-month divisor gives 32.804 stb/d, which the simulator applies over 31 days and produces 1.8 percent too much oil in that month alone.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'Why do mean-month divisor errors not cancel over three years?', '["Because they compound, since each month''s error shifts the starting cumulative for the next month.", "Because production declines through the history, so later months carry more weight in the total.", "Because they follow the calendar: every 31-day month is over-produced and every 30-day month under-produced.", "Because leap years add a day to February that the 30.4375 divisor has already accounted for twice within the same calendar year."]'::jsonb, 2, 'February is over-produced by nearly ten percent. A history match whose residuals correlate with month length has this bug and nothing else, which makes the symptom distinctive.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'One line of arithmetic catches every divisor mistake in a schedule. What is it?', '["Multiply each rate by the days its period spans, sum over wells and periods, and confirm the total reproduces the ledger''s own.", "Compare the schedule''s first and last dates against the ledger''s, confirming the two span the same interval.", "Count the WCONHIST blocks against the ledger months, which catches a dropped or duplicated period.", "Confirm every well appears in every period, which catches keyword persistence repeating a stale rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It catches every divisor mistake at once and costs one line. The count and persistence checks are both worth doing and neither would catch a wrong divisor, because the counts would be right and the rates wrong.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'Both Ekene injectors found the sand below the contact. What does that do in the model?', '["Their cells are excluded from the oil in place, which is why the deck''s oil cell count differs from the booking''s.", "Their cells initialise at a water saturation of one, so they inject water into water and support the oil indirectly.", "Their completions must be restricted to layers above the contact, or the simulator refuses the well at initialisation.", "Their injection is booked as out of zone, which is where the waterflood course''s twelve percent figure comes from."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oil is up-structure and the injection supports it by pressure and by displacing water toward it. That is the two-jobs distinction falling out of the deck''s geometry rather than being asserted separately.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Take the tier''s six graded fields together. What is true of every one of them?', '["Each requires a conversion between the deck''s field units and the metric units the field was mapped in.", "Each is computed from the deck alone, with the booking and the well database serving only as context.", "Each depends on the calibrated regional mean, which is why the prompt states it rather than leaving it derived.", "Each holds two sources against each other."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The deck against the booking, the deck against the well database, the design against the correlation. Only one involves a unit conversion, four need a source outside the deck, and the calibration parameter belongs to the Expert tier.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'The structure is described as calibrated rather than validated. What would a validation have needed?', '["A formal review by a second engineer, with the acceptance criterion agreed before any tuning began.", "Something the model was not tuned to reproduce, such as a seventh well whose top the surface then predicts.", "An independent volumetric booking computed on the deck''s own frame, so the two could be compared like with like.", "A sensitivity run at a shallower and a deeper regional mean, showing the booking falls inside the resulting spread."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene has none spare: all six wells went into the interpolation. That is the honest closing line and it tells the next person what to go and get. The sensitivity run is worth doing and tests nothing the model was not already tuned against.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Which caveat concerns precision rather than accuracy?', '["The deck carries fifteen digits while the production history behind it is an allocation good to a few percent.", "The structure was calibrated against the booking, so the agreement between them is arranged rather than independently arrived at.", "One saturation region asserts a 607 md layer and a 102 md layer flow alike, which is not what rock does.", "The gas-oil table has no measurement behind it, so nothing in the course is graded on its three parameters."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A monthly per-well oil volume is typically good to a few percent and the deck carries it to fifteen figures, because that is what a double holds rather than what the measurement supports.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'What does the Expert tier assume from this one?', '["The kriging parameters and the well positions, since the Expert tier re-intersects deviated trajectories against the very same grid.", "The PVT and rock curve provenance, since its calibration adjusts both to match the volumetric booking.", "The reconciliation and that it was arranged, and the cell-centre rule for assigning a cell to oil or water.", "The naming rule and the completion strategy, since it adds a new well to the model that already exists."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Its calibration module picks the reconciliation up directly. Every volumetric statement there depends on knowing that Eclipse decides a cell''s fluid by its centre depth and that the alternative gives a different and equally defensible number.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'What is a deck read on its own always?', '["Incomplete, because a deck cannot express the provenance of any of the numbers that it carries.", "Self-consistent, because it is one file.", "Ambiguous, because the same numbers describe several reservoirs depending on the conventions assumed.", "Sufficient, because everything a simulator needs is in it and nothing outside it changes what the model does."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is why this tier''s whole method is comparison. A deck IS sufficient for a simulator and that is exactly the problem, because sufficiency for a solver is not evidence about a field.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'What four-line provenance note does the tier ask for?', '["One line each for the grid, the fluids, the wells and the schedule, matching the sections that carry data.", "One line each for the author, the date, the simulator version and the study the deck belongs to.", "One line each for the six wells, the volumetric booking, the SCAL fit and the flood ledger, naming every external source the deck drew on.", "One line each for the structure, the volume, the oil and the rock curves, saying where each of the four actually came from."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene''s would read: structure kriged from six mapped tops with a calibrated regional mean, volume matched to the booking under the cell-centre convention, oil designed rather than correlated, rock curves one Corey fit applied everywhere.'),
  ('sim', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'The Expert tier is described as moving from auditing to authoring. What follows?', '["It stops reading the deck and starts reading results, which is where a study''s conclusions actually live.", "It moves from the static model to the dynamic one, so the questions become about flow rather than geometry.", "Every decision must now be made and defended rather than found and checked, which is why its central lesson is about reporting.", "It begins running the deck, which is the step the course has deliberately deferred until its final module."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A trajectory becomes a completion list, a database becomes a schedule, and a free parameter is tuned and the tuning reported. The Expert tier has a results module and grades none of it, and the course never runs a deck.');
